    "result_text": "Green. Here's what I changed and why it satisfies the spec with minimal structural impact.\n\n**Two files touched:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 rewrote the single `service` method. The customerCode is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`:\n   - `REGION` = region derived from the postcode, reusing the existing `Locality.of(null, postcode)` (postcode-only region lookup, `UNKNOWN` when absent/out of range) \u2014 no new region table.\n   - `HASH8` = first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName` (telephone is already E.164-normalized upstream in the pipeline).\n   - Dropped the per-city sequence loop entirely, so the `OwnerRepository` dependency is gone and the method is now branch-free (CC 1).\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the locality derivation now reads the `REGION` prefix off the new `customerCode` identity (with a fall-back to the old `Locality.of(city, postcode)` only when the code is missing), moving it onto the region-and-hash identity.\n\n**No change needed** for the membership number, its Luhn check digit, and the create audit record: each already reads `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so they automatically reflect the new region-and-hash value once `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` changed. The audit line confirms it: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\nBoth changed methods stay simple (CC 1), sit in low-complexity surroundings, and no method or class grew.\n\nThe acceptance test (`customerCode` matches `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`) and the full suite pass.",
